.TH ANSIDRAMA 1 "2026-07-07" "ansidrama" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ansidrama \- assemble or record a terminal session into a crisp animated WebP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ansidrama encode
.I config.toml
.RB [ \-o
.IR out.webp ]
.RB [ \-\-dump\-png
.IR dir ]
.br
.B ansidrama record
.I config.toml
.RB [ \-o
.IR out.webp ]
.RB [ \-\-dump\-png
.IR dir ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
AnsiDrama
turns a terminal session into a small, lossless, looping animated WebP.
It renders each frame itself \(em parsing the terminal cell grid and
rasterizing it with bundled monospace fonts \(em JetBrains Mono for text, Symbols
Nerd Font for Nerd Font icons, JuliaMono for Unicode symbol blocks \(em
hand\-painting box\-drawing and
block glyphs so characters reach the exact cell edges and tile seamlessly.
Every run is deterministic: the same script produces the same bytes. There are
no runtime dependencies \(em a single static binary, no browser, no ffmpeg, no
tmux.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B encode
Assemble a WebP from a list of captured ANSI snapshots and/or synthetic title
cards, each held for a duration.
.TP
.B record
Drive a command inside an embedded terminal (a PTY plus a VT parser) per a
scene script, capturing one frame per key, per typed character, and per mouse
cell\-step.
.B record
is available on unix platforms only.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out " " \fIpath\fR
Output WebP path. Overrides the
.B out
key in the config.
.TP
.BR \-\-dump\-png " " \fIdir\fR
Also write each rendered frame as a PNG into
.I dir
(for debugging).
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is TOML. The
.B out
key is resolved relative to the config file's directory.
.SS "encode config"
Top level:
.BR cols ", " rows ", " font_px ", " card_font_px ", " card_subtitle_px ", "
.BR max_fps ", " out .
Each
.B [[frame]]
has either
.B file
(path to a captured
.I .ansi
snapshot, relative to the config) or
.B card
(a title card), plus
.B hold_cs
(hold in centiseconds).
.SS "record config"
Top level:
.BR launch " (the shell command line), "
.BR cols ", " rows ", " font_px ", " card_font_px ", " card_subtitle_px ", "
.BR max_fps ", " out ", " env ", " startup_ms ", "
.BR sample_ms ", " change_ms ", " stable_ms ", " persist_ms ", "
.BR wait_cap_ms ", " await_ms ", " realtime ", " max_capture_mb ", "
.BR type_cs ", " move_cs ", " quit_keys ", " cursor .
Each
.B [[scene]]
performs exactly one action \(em
.BR keys ", " text ", " click ", " drag ", " scroll ", or " card " \(em"
plus timing
.RB ( hold_cs ", " type_cs ", " move_cs ),
and optionally
.BR await ", " await_ms ", or " animated .
A scene expands into many frames: one captured per key, per typed character,
and per mouse cell\-step.
.PP
.B record
samples the terminal grid continuously rather than guessing from a quiet
terminal. The sampling keys, all in milliseconds and defaulting to the
spec's values:
.TP
.B sample_ms " (10)"
Grid snapshot interval.
.TP
.B change_ms " (150)"
Grace given for the app's first reaction to an input; spent in full only by
an input that draws nothing at all.
.TP
.B stable_ms " (40)"
How long the grid must hold still before the recorder treats a screen as
settled and moves on to the next input.
.TP
.B persist_ms " (40)"
How long a state must persist before it earns a frame in the assembled
movie, filtering out one-frame flicker between two real screens.
.B stable_ms
and
.B persist_ms
default to the same value but answer different questions \(em when to act,
versus what to keep. Raise
.B stable_ms
for a scene that redraws in two visible stages; lower
.B persist_ms
for a scene with a fast animation.
.TP
.B wait_cap_ms " (3000)"
Bound on a wait with no
.BR await .
.TP
.B await_ms " (5000)"
Default timeout for an
.BR await ;
overridable per scene.
.TP
.B realtime " (false)"
Play the whole recording at measured time instead of pacing on stability.
.TP
.B max_capture_mb " (256)"
Backstop on accumulated grid memory.
.PP
A scene's
.B await
declares what its finished screen looks like, replacing a timing guess with
a fact: either
.B await = "text"
(whole\-screen match) or
.B await = { find = "text", row = -1 }
(row\-scoped; a negative row counts from the bottom). If the pattern never
matches within
.BR await_ms ,
the run aborts, naming the pattern and showing the last screen \(em never a
silently wrong frame.
.B await
is rejected at config load (not silently ignored) on a
.B card
scene, on a scene with
.B animated = true ,
or anywhere under top\-level
.BR realtime " = true" ,
since in each of those cases it could never be honoured.
.B animated
marks a screen that never holds still (spinner, clock, progress bar): the
scene dwells for
.B hold_cs
and captures whatever is there instead of waiting for stability.
.PP
Migration:
.B settle_ms
and
.B react_ms
are gone; delete them. A config that still carries either now fails to
parse.
.SS "title cards"
A
.B card
is a silent\-movie intertitle:
.BR text " (or " lines "), " fg ", " bg ", " bold ", " border ", " font_px ", " subtitle_px .
Colours are \fI#rrggbb\fR, \fI#rgb\fR, or a basic name (black white red green
blue yellow grey).
.SS "window chrome"
An optional
.B [chrome]
table (in either config) draws window chrome and padding around the screen:
.BR style " (" none ", " macos ", " linux "), " title ", " padding ", " bar ", " text .
.BR style " " macos " gives macOS traffic-light dots and a centered title;"
.BR linux " gives a single close button and a left title;"
.BR padding " insets the cells with the terminal background (works with " none ")."
.SH "KEY NAMES"
Named keys accepted in a scene's
.B keys
list (tmux\-style):
.B Enter Return Tab BTab Escape Esc Space BSpace Backspace Up Down Right Left
.B Home End PageUp PPage PageDown NPage Insert IC Delete DC F1\-F12 .
Modifiers:
.BR C- " (control), " M- " (meta/alt), " S- " (shift) \(em e.g. " C-c ", " M-x ", " S-Tab .
A single character is sent literally; a value beginning with ESC passes through
as a raw escape sequence.
.SH EXAMPLES
Assemble frames listed in a config into a WebP:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ansidrama encode demo.toml \-o out.webp
.EE
.RE
.PP
Record an app, dumping each frame as a PNG for inspection:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ansidrama record record.toml \-\-dump\-png frames/
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
The output WebP (from
.BR \-o " or the " out " config key)."
With
.BR \-\-dump\-png " " \fIdir\fR ,
one
.I frameNNNN.png
per frame, plus
.I manifest.tsv
mapping each frame back to the scene and input that produced it.
Columns:
.BR frame ", "
.BR scene ", "
.B input
(the input's ordinal within its scene, or
.RB \(dq \- \(dq
when none),
.B kind
(one of
.BR input\-driven ", " app\-driven ", or " card ),
and
.BR hold_cs .
An
.B input\-driven
frame is an input's settled result, held for the duration the script
authored; an
.B app\-driven
frame is the app moving on its own after the input already settled, held
for its own measured duration.
